    "prompt": "## Incident\nDataset `raw_payments` is stale. datamend did not auto-rerun it because a guardrail failed:\nidempotent: load_raw_payments idempotent=False\n\n## Context\n- Job: `load_raw_payments`, file `pipelines/load_raw_payments.py`. Each job is a Python file with `run(con, now, mode)`.\n  `con` is a DuckDB connection, `now` is the load timestamp, `mode` is \"rerun\" or \"backfill\".\n- Upstreams: none (source table)\n- Downstream, rebuilt after this job: payments, revenue_daily\n- Helpers available in `datamend/pipeline.py` are NOT in this sandbox; use plain SQL via `con`.\n\n## Task\n1. Read `pipelines/load_raw_payments.py` and the downstream jobs in `pipelines/` to understand the impact.\n2. Find why rerunning `load_raw_payments` is unsafe.\n3. Edit only `pipelines/load_raw_payments.py` so that running it any number of times, in either mode, leaves\n   `raw_payments` with the same rows as running it once. Keep the schema and columns.\n4. Reply with ONLY this JSON object, no prose around it:\n{\"root_cause\": \"...\", \"fix\": \"...\", \"rationale\": \"why this fix and not another\",\n  \"risk\": \"low|medium|high, and what could still go wrong\", \"confidence\": 0.0}\n",
    "explanation": {
     "root_cause": "load_raw_payments did a plain INSERT of the full 200-row export (ids 0..199) into raw_payments on every run, with no delete or dedupe. Each rerun or backfill appended another 200 rows, so ids were duplicated. Downstream, payments copies raw_payments as is, and revenue_daily sums payments.amount, so revenue would be multiplied by the number of runs.",
     "fix": "In pipelines/load_raw_payments.py, the load now runs in one transaction. It deletes all rows from raw_payments, inserts the 200-row export, and commits. It rolls back and re-raises on error. The schema and columns are unchanged.",
     "rationale": "The export is always the full day's snapshot, so a full replace is the natural idempotent load. It also removes duplicates already left by earlier reruns, which an insert-only-missing-ids approach would keep. The transaction means a failed run can't leave the table empty or half-loaded. I did not add a primary key or change the schema, since that is out of scope. I did not use a per-day partition delete, since the table has no date key apart from _loaded_at.",
     "risk": "low. Rows are identical across runs except _loaded_at, which is set to the new run's timestamp. A check that compares _loaded_at would see a difference. If the real export ever stops being the full set of ids, the full delete would drop the rows missing from it. I could not run the code, so the DuckDB transaction handling is unvalidated. If the runner already wraps the job in a transaction, the nested BEGIN would fail.",
